{"input_text": "Question: Having a function $f:\mathbb{N}\rightarrow\mathbb{N}$: 
 $$f(n)=\left\{
   \begin{array}{lcc}
    n-3 & if & n \geq 1000
    \\f(f(n+6)) & if & n < 1000
   \end{array}
  \right\}$$
Calculate the exact value of $f(1)$ by hand. 
 Is there an easy way to solve it? 

Answer: You have $f(1)=f(f(7))$, $f(7)=f(f(13))$ and so on. The numbers $1, 7, 13,... $ form a sequence with general term $a_{n}=6n-5$. So if we want $6n-5\ge 1000$ we need $ n\ge 168$. This gives us for $n=167$, $f(997)=f(f(1003))=f(1000)=997$. Thus $f(991)=f(f(997))=997$ and so on. Therefore $f(1)=997$. 
